---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import duoImage from '../assets/live/manchesters-live-02.jpg';
import rachelImage from '../assets/shows/rachel-headshot.jpeg';
import peterImage from '../assets/shows/peter-headshot.jpg';
export const prerender = true;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About us"
  description="Rachel and Peter Manchester met at sixth form, trained at two of the world's most prestigious drama schools, and have spent a decade performing together on the world's most exclusive cruise ships."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="spotlight spotlight--one" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="spotlight spotlight--two" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="page-hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">About us</p>
        <h1>Our mission: <br/><span class="accent-word">make people happier</span> through interesting &amp; <span class="accent-word">highly entertaining</span> shows.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          We bring the stories and beauty of well-known music and musicians to life — aiming to
          educate and entertain with the stories behind the songs, while giving every audience a
          great time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="story">
    <div class="wrap story-grid">
      <div class="story-copy">
        <h2>Their story</h2>
        <p>
          Rachel and Peter are a married couple who (amazingly) love working together. They met at
          sixth form college, age sixteen, in the North West of England, where they sang in the
          same choirs, played in the same bands and appeared in the same theatre shows — but
          didn't get together until after they went to Drama School.
        </p>
        <p>
          They trained at two of the most prestigious drama schools in the world: Rachel studied
          acting at Guildford School of Acting, and Peter studied acting at the Royal Central
          School of Speech and Drama. After graduating, they both worked extensively in theatre
          around the UK and Europe, appearing in the West End and in shows and theatres including
          Les Mis&eacute;rables, The Crucible, The Recruiting Officer and A Midsummer Night's Dream,
          at venues including the London Palladium, the Royal Albert Hall, the Barbican, the Donmar
          Warehouse and the Theatre Royal Bath.
        </p>
        <p>
          It took them three years to realise they should be performing together, and that they
          loved travelling the world as a team. Since then, they've never looked back — continuing
          to travel, meet interesting people, and perform in new places, including a decade
          onboard the world's most exclusive cruise ships.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="story-media">
        <Image src={duoImage} alt="Rachel and Peter Manchester performing live" width={900} height={1100} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="performers">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The performers</p>
      <h2>Two actors, singers and multi-instrumentalists</h2>
      <div class="performer-grid">
        <article class="performer">
          <Image src={rachelImage} alt="Rachel and Peter Manchester" width={700} height={700} />
          <h3>Rachel Manchester</h3>
          <p class="role">Vocals, flute, woodwinds</p>
          <p>
            After graduating from Guildford School of Acting, Rachel performed in a vast amount of
            classical theatre including Sense and Sensibility and The Crucible, at the Theatre
            Royal Bath and in Stratford-upon-Avon, in Casanova in Venice, and on a UK tour of The Snow Queen. She has been singing for many years and has
            worked for a decade alongside Peter as a professional entertainer. She is inherently
            musical and has played the flute since she was very young, took up the saxophone at a
            later age, and now even plays wind synth.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="performer">
          <Image src={peterImage} alt="Rachel and Peter Manchester" width={700} height={700} />
          <h3>Peter Manchester</h3>
          <p class="role">Vocals, guitars, keys</p>
          <p>
            Following graduation from the Royal Central School of Speech and Drama, Peter went on
            to work in Les Mis&eacute;rables in the West End and on tour, and in many more musicals
            including 20th Century Boy and Mods and Rox, in plays including A Midsummer Night's Dream,
            and The Recruiting Officer at venues including the Donmar Warehouse
            and the London Palladium for the Royal Variety Show, the Classic Music Awards and the Royal Albert Hall. Peter
            is a self-taught guitarist, picking up his first guitar at age four, followed shortly
            by double bass, piano and various other stringed instruments — he's been singing since
            he was a youngster.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta">
    <div class="wrap cta-inner">
      <h2>See more about the shows</h2>
      <a class="button button--primary" href="/shows">Explore the shows</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-hero {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding-top: 3rem;
  }

  .page-hero-copy {
    position: relative;
    max-width: 50rem;
  }

  /* Two soft, slow-drifting gel lights — the same "arena rock poster"
     spotlight treatment as the homepage hero, filling the open space
     beside the hero copy on wide screens instead of leaving it bare. */
  .spotlight {
    position: absolute;
    width: 28rem;
    height: 28rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(70px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
    opacity: 0.24;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Fixed rem offsets (not percentages) so the glow lands in the same
     visible spot regardless of how tall the heading makes this
     section — a percentage-based offset would get pushed mostly out
     of view whenever the heading wraps to extra lines. */
  .spotlight--one {
    top: 1rem;
    right: -6rem;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, var(--coral), transparent 70%);
    animation: page-hero-drift-one 24s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  .spotlight--two {
    top: 12rem;
    right: 4rem;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, var(--cyan), transparent 70%);
    animation: page-hero-drift-two 28s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes page-hero-drift-one {
    0%, 100% {
      transform: translate(0, 0);
    }
    50% {
      transform: translate(-5%, 8%);
    }
  }

  @keyframes page-hero-drift-two {
    0%, 100% {
      transform: translate(0, 0);
    }
    50% {
      transform: translate(6%, -8%);
    }
  }

  .story-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.3fr 1fr;
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .story-media :global(img) {
    border-radius: 14px;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .performer-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(18rem, 1fr));
    gap: 2rem;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }

  .performer :global(img) {
    display: block;
    border-radius: 14px;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 300px;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 auto 1.25rem;
  }

  .role {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .cta-inner {
    text-align: center;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .story-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
